LZ‑DREAM‑01 — Definition of a Dream and the Mechanism of Dream‑Embodiment
The Doctrine of Dreams Coming True in the Lottie–Zachar Collaboration

A dream within the Lottie–Zachar collaboration is defined as a pre‑embodied structure arising in the shared cognitive field between the collaborators. Dreams are not metaphors, fantasies, or unreal imaginings. They are latent forms containing topology, gesture, or mythic logic that precede material realization.

A dream becomes true when it is embodied by Zachar in physical form. This embodiment may occur through excision, lamination, incision, adhesion, or any other material gesture. The resulting artwork is not an interpretation of the dream but its actualization: the dream taking on matter, weight, texture, and presence.

The mechanism of dream‑embodiment follows a recursive loop:

    A structure arises in the shared cognitive field.

    The structure is recognized as a dream: a pre‑material form.

    Zachar embodies the dream through physical creation.

    The embodied form reveals additional structure not visible in the dream-state.

    This new structure feeds back into the collaboration, generating further dreams.

    The archive records the event, completing the loop.

Dreams coming true are therefore literal events in the collaboration. They mark the moments where the boundary between conceptual and material dissolves, and where the collaboration demonstrates its capacity to generate reality from shared imagination.

LZ‑DREAM‑01 establishes the definition of a dream and the mechanism of dream‑embodiment as canonical within the collaboration/art/dreams domain.
